{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# rebar-debug final report\n\n## Problem\n\n`rebar-root-disk-pressure` breached on the `rebar-gerrit` host: the 30-GiB root volume climbed\n71% → 90% over ~an hour on 2026-08-17, the alarm fired at 21:51 PDT, `reviewbot_healthy` dipped\nto 0 at 22:05, and the disk then dropped abruptly to 48%. Story `sour-plugugly-rhino`'s\npressure-triggered Docker prune had not prevented it.\n\n## Proven root cause\n\nTwo causes; both are needed, because one explains the **growth rate** and the other the\n**failure to reclaim**.\n\n**RC-B (repaired here) — the snapshot janitor's reclaim triggers are structurally unreachable\non this volume.** `src/rebar/_snapshot/janitor.py:53`\n`DEFAULT_FREE_WATERMARK_BYTES = 2 * 1024**3` is an **absolute**, disk-size-blind floor. On a\n30-GiB root it is crossed only at **93.3% used** — above the 85% alarm\n(`infra/terraform/monitoring_autodeploy.tf:226`) and above the 90% peak actually reached. The\n7-day cold-trim (`janitor.py:55`) exceeds the container's lifetime (23 recreations in 4 days),\nand the byte-total backstop that ADR 0005 and the module docstring both promise is not\nimplemented in `_gc_pass`. The janitor **is** started\n(`src/rebar/review_bot/app.py:134-136`) and ran every 300 s throughout — it correctly evicted\nnothing.\n\n**RC-A (split to `smart-disloyal-naga`) — the growth source.** Every review materializes a full\n~620 MiB / 69,984-file copy of the ticket tree (`src/rebar/llm/gate_source.py:99` →\n`_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py:675-703`, `git read-tree` + `checkout-index`, no hardlinks, no\ndelta, no release), keyed on the tickets tip — which advanced **1,374 times** in the 10-hour\nincident window, with consecutive commits differing by **exactly 1 file**. ~0.85 GiB per review\nmodels 2.5-10 GiB/h against the measured 4.4 GiB/h.\n\nThe store is on the root disk because `REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR` is set **nowhere**\n(`docker-compose.yml`, `Dockerfile.reviewbot`, `fetch-secrets.sh`), so `store_root()`\n(`repo_snapshot.py:113`) falls through to `tempfile.gettempdir()` — the container's writable\nlayer, which no volume mount covers.\n\n## Proof\n\n- **E1 (runtime, decisive).** Drove the real `run_gc()` with injected free space on a 30-GiB\n  volume: **0 evictions at 4.50 GiB free (85% used) and at 3.00 GiB free (90% used)**; first\n  eviction only past 93.3%.\n- **E5 (falsifies the rival).** Ticket hypothesis 2 said the bytes were buildkit cache/images\n  from autodeploy rebuilds. Build artifacts cannot exist without a build. Across 12 maximal\n  build-free intervals, **10 grew faster than 0.5 GiB/h (median +1.12, max +3.93)**, and the\n  only flat one (+0.00) is where *review* activity nearly stopped. **Rejected.**\n- Three further nails: the 12.6-GiB reclaim exceeds the whole 5 GB buildkit ceiling;\n  `--keep-storage` leaves a variable residue so 23 prunes cannot converge on the identical\n  **48%** floor observed; and with `deploy_deferrals = 0` from 20:30-21:50 the no-op branch\n  *was* being taken, so a pressure prune fired ~every 10 min from 21:25 while the disk climbed\n  80% → 90% straight through them.\n- **E3/E4.** All 23 reclaims follow a `BOT_PATHS` commit; none occurs in a build-free window.\n  Deferrals began 20-30 min *after* the alarm floor was crossed, so the drain gate is an\n  aggravating bystander, not the cause.\n\n## Test\n\n`tests/unit/test_snapshot_janitor.py` — 7 new cases. Observed **RED on the assertions** (not on\na construction error: a field-only scaffold was used to prove the failure reason), e.g.\n*\"at 85% used on a 30-GiB volume the janitor must reclaim BEFORE the rebar-root-disk-pressure\nalarm fires, not after\"*. The file was moved out of the fixer's tree entirely — the\nimplementation was written against a frozen contract card only. It then went **GREEN (24/24)**\nagainst that held-out oracle, and the **exact-defect mutation** (forcing `_space_thresholds`\nback to the absolute-only watermark) returned the same 4 cases to RED. The assertions are on\n`GcResult.evicted` / `skipped_grace` / entry existence — observable output of the public\n`run_gc` API, so they are not change-detectors.\n\n## Fix\n\nAdd a second, **volume-relative** term to the janitor's free-space trigger. The trigger is the\n**larger** of the absolute floor and `free_watermark_pct` percent of the volume, so raising one\nterm can never weaken the other; once a pass starts it runs on to a target\n`RECLAIM_TARGET_MARGIN_PCT` (5) points higher — a fixed internal constant, deliberately not an\noperator knob — so it overshoots the threshold rather than stalling on it and re-firing every\ninterval. The percentage is **whole integer points**, matching the janitor's existing int-only\nconfig seam; a fractional value would be truncated to 0 by `_snapshot_int` and silently disable\nthe term (this was caught by the plan-review gate before implementation). It defaults to **0\n(off)**, so every existing deployment is bit-identical; the review-bot service opts in at\n**20** (reclaim at 80% used), matching `autodeploy.sh`'s `DISK_PRESSURE_PCT=80`.\n\nThis addresses the mechanism, not the symptom: it makes the documented ordering\n(`infra/runbooks/review-bot-ops.md:500-508` — the janitor reclaims, the alarm is the backstop)\nactually hold on a small volume.\n\nFiles: `src/rebar/_snapshot/janitor.py`, `src/rebar/_config_sources.py`,\n`infra/compose/docker-compose.yml`, `docs/config.md`, `docs/repo-snapshot-gates.md`,\n`docs/env-vars.md`, `tests/unit/test_snapshot_janitor.py`.\n\n## Scope & safety\n\n- **Caller-dependency sweep:** `run_gc`/`_gc_pass`'s only behavioural consumer is the\n  review-bot lifespan via `start_background_janitor()` → `JanitorConfig.from_env()`. With the\n  new knob defaulting to 0 its behaviour is unchanged unless the env var is set — **incidental\n  usage only**, no behavioural dependency broken.\n- **Restoration, not creation:** no new entry point, command, or user-visible artifact; it\n  restores an ordering the runbook already specifies.\n- **No new dependency.**\n- **No test weakened.** `_snapshot_int` was refactored (its last parameter became the defaults\n  mapping, indexed by the key it already used) purely to keep `_config_sources.py` under the\n  repo's 800-line hard cap after adding the entry — behaviour-preserving, and the helper is\n  private with no callers outside its module. `_reverify_pass` was extracted verbatim from\n  `_gc_pass` to stay under the complexity ceiling.\n- Gates: `make lint`, `make typecheck` (567 files), `check_complexity_baseline.py --check`\n  (`new=0 increased=0`), module sizes 432 / 791 — all green.\n\n## Siblings\n\n- **Construct sweep:** the construct is \"an absolute byte threshold compared against free disk\n  space\". `shutil.disk_usage` / `statvfs` appear **nowhere else** in `src/` — no siblings.\n- **Parity sweep:** the rule is \"a reclaim threshold must sit below the operator's alarm floor\".\n  Surfaces: the snapshot janitor (**fixed**); `autodeploy.sh`'s `DISK_PRESSURE_PCT=80`\n  (**already correct**, below 85%); the 50-GiB `/var/gerrit` data volume, alarmed at 85% by\n  `rebar-gerrit-data-disk-high` but with **no reclaim surface at all** — **accepted**, with the\n  rationale already documented at `docker-compose.yml:210-216` (append-only `usage.jsonl` at\n  ~100-200 MB/year against 44 GiB free). Note this becomes load-bearing if anyone \"fixes\" the\n  root-disk problem by relocating the snapshot store onto `/var/gerrit`: that converts a\n  root-disk incident into a data-disk one, which would take Gerrit itself down.\n\n## Residual risk & prevention\n\n- **`smart-disloyal-naga`** (ticket filed) — RC-A: the ~620 MiB-per-review ticket-tree copy on\n  a cache key with no reuse. Unbounded by construction; this fix bounds the *store*, not the\n  *rate*.\n- **`itchy-arbored-peccary`** (ticket filed) — the ADR-promised byte-total backstop is absent\n  from `_gc_pass`, and `materialize_tickets` never calls `add_bytes`, so the dominant entries\n  are byte-accounted at zero. Also the prerequisite for defaulting `free_watermark_pct` on\n  everywhere (Option B in the plan) rather than opting in per deployment.\n- **`uninspired-gabardine-pilchard`** (ticket filed) — `AUTODEPLOY_DISK_PRESSURE` is emitted as\n  a journal marker but published as no metric (story 28f9 scoped it out), so \"did the reclaim\n  gate run?\" is answerable only with host access. This directly lengthened this investigation.\n- **Accepted:** the 7-day cold-trim is dead code on a container recreated hourly. Harmless\n  here, but it means the space watermark is the *only* live trigger — which is precisely what\n  made this incident possible.\n\n## Replication\n\nRe-ran the incident's CloudWatch series through the real janitor using the **deployed\nconfiguration path** (`JanitorConfig.from_env()` with `REBAR_GATE_FREE_WATERMARK_PCT=20`, as\ncompose now sets it) on a 30-GiB volume. Reclamation now engages at the **82%** sample —\n**below the 85% alarm floor** — where pre-fix every sample from 69% through 90% reclaimed\nnothing. The original report no longer reproduces.\n\n## Not established (host access blocked)\n\n`ssm:SendCommand` is denied in this session, so no host `du` / `docker system df` / journald\nevidence is in this record. RC-A's dominance rests on measured artifact sizes, measured ref\nchurn, the code path, and the rate match rather than a direct measurement of the live\ndirectory. The one read-only host command still wanted: `docker ps --size` plus\n`du -xh --max-depth=1 /tmp` inside `compose-review-bot-1`. It would confirm the split, not\nchange the conclusion."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787177852885883001,"uuid":"55c9964a-bdae-4718-a6a1-225d1dd368c8"}